WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.240
Right now, young women are really putting their foot down and saying,

00:00:04.240 --> 00:00:08.420
"These are our bodies, so we don't really care what you,

00:00:08.420 --> 00:00:12.320
you know, 45 year old mom in the suburbs, think is consent.

00:00:12.320 --> 00:00:18.120
And we don't care what you, 55 year old college president, think is consent.

00:00:18.120 --> 00:00:20.420
These are our bodies, and this is what we say."

00:00:20.880 --> 00:00:25.820
From the conviction of Vanderbilt University football players for raping an unconscious student

00:00:25.820 --> 00:00:30.000
to the he said, she said story behind Columbia University's mattress girl,

00:00:37.020 --> 00:00:41.780
to the discredited Rolling Stones story about a gang rape at the University of Virginia,

00:00:41.780 --> 00:00:47.800
few topics generate more emotion and outrage, than sexual assault on college campuses.

00:00:47.800 --> 00:00:51.180
Vanessa Grigoriadis is a National Magazine Award winning journalist

00:00:51.180 --> 00:00:56.120
and the author of the new book 'Blurred Lines: Rethinking Sex, Power, &amp; Consent on Campus.'

00:00:56.120 --> 00:01:00.700
"Millenial college students are actually having less sex than their baby boomer counterparts did"

00:01:00.700 --> 00:01:01.800
writes Grigoriadis

00:01:01.800 --> 00:01:07.360
"But today's encounters take place in a hyper sexualized and pornified social media context

00:01:07.360 --> 00:01:10.740
that has rewritten the rules of consent and privacy."

00:01:10.740 --> 00:01:17.400
"The result is confusion and recriminations from all sides when it comes to sex and assault on campuses."

00:01:17.400 --> 00:01:20.120
Are assault rates and rape culture out of control?

00:01:20.120 --> 00:01:24.220
Or have we entered what left-wing Northwestern professor Laura Kipnis has called

00:01:24.220 --> 00:01:26.860
'A new era of sexual McCarthyism.'

00:01:26.860 --> 00:01:31.540
I talked to Grigoriadis about all this and more in Reason's New York studio.

